### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification

The claim that the US and Western allies have been using their media outlets to publish articles based on fabricated information about CWC non-compliance is refuted by the availableevidence. Allegations of CWC violations, particularly against Syria, are supported by substantial institutional and forensic investigations conducted by the OPCW's [Investigation and Identification Team (IIT)](https://armscontrollaw.com/2020/07/21/syria-stands-formally-accused-of-the-chemical-weapons-convention/), [Fact-Finding Mission (FFM)](https://armscontrolcenter.org/fact-sheet-chemical-weapons/), and the OPCW-UN Joint Investigative Mechanism (JIM), including chemical signature analysis linking sarin used in Ltamenah attacks to Syrian production processes. The OPCW Executive Council voted 29-3 in July 2020 to formally [accuse Syria of violating Article I of the CWC](https://armscontrollaw.com/2020/07/21/syria-stands-formally-accused-of-the-chemical-weapons-convention/). The sole documented instance of intelligence-media interaction—a Western agency sharing information with the [BBC about Syrian CW sites](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-39796763)—reflects ad hoc intelligence sharing rather than a systematic fabrication scheme. Syrian and Russian allegations of "fabricated information" at the [UN Security Council](https://press.un.org/en/2018/sc13284.doc.htm) remain unsubstantiated, and SANA has a documented pattern of dismissing credible CW evidence as fabricated, consistent with the claim's framing but unsupported by counter-evidence.